%N A401688 First member of the least set of 6 consecutive primes such that the sum of each pair of consecutive primes in this set is a multiple of n.
%C A401688 Since prime(i) + prime(i+1) = 0 (mod m), it follows that prime(i), prime(i+1), ..., prime(n) must alternate between two opposite classes of residues mod m: x, -x, x, -x, ... (mod m) or -x, x, -x, x, ... (mod m).
%C A401688 The "Conjecture 92" could be restated as follows: for every m and every k>1 there exists at least a constellation of k consecutive primes that alternately occupy two opposite residue classes mod m.
%H A401688 Paolo P. Lava, <a href="/A401688/b401688.txt">Table of n, a(n) for n = 1..50</a>
%H A401688 Carlos Rivera, <a href="https://www.primepuzzles.net/conjectures/conj_092.htm">Conjecture 92. For any integer m there is at least one set of consecutive primes...</a>, The Prime Puzzles and Problems Connection.
%e A401688 a(4) = 131. The least 6 consecutive primes are 131, 137, 139, 149, 151, 157:
%e A401688   131 + 137 = 268 and 268/4 = 67;
%e A401688   137 + 139 = 276 and 276/4 = 69;
%e A401688   139 + 149 = 288 and 288/4 = 72;
%e A401688   149 + 151 = 300 and 300/4 = 75;
%e A401688   151 + 157 = 308 and 308/4 = 77.
%e A401688 a(10) = 6037. The least 6 consecutive primes are 6037, 6043, 6047, 6053, 6067, 6073:
%e A401688   6037 + 6043 = 12080 and 12080/10 = 1208;
%e A401688   6043 + 6047 = 12090 and 12090/10 = 1209;
%e A401688   6047 + 6053 = 12100 and 12100/10 = 1210;
%e A401688   6053 + 6067 = 12120 and 12120/10 = 1212;
%e A401688   6067 + 6073 = 12140 and 12140/10 = 1214.
%Y A401688 Cf. A254862 (2 consecutive), A382698 (3 consecutive), A382699 (4 consecutive), A382700 (5 consecutive).
